  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1159 AM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nSynoptically, the region is under the influence of H5 ridging \nand surface high pressure across the southeast CONUS. Another \nmid- level ridge is located across the Desert Southwest, while \nbroad troughing is present across the Northern Tier. A deep 996 \nmb surface low is located over Hudson Bay, with a weak/diffuse \ntrailing cold front oriented from NE to SW across the Great \nLakes and Central Great Plains. \n\nThe surface high over the southeast CONUS will bring continued\ndry and seasonable temperature and humidity values today through\nWednesday. More shallow predawn fog development is likely\nWednesday morning in low spots and near bodies of water.\nBeginning early Thursday morning, the cold front to our\nnorthwest will attempt (but fail) to cross through the area and\nbecome stationary across southern/eastern MO into central IL.\nThe closer proximity of the front will initiate periods of\nscattered showers and thunderstorms. \n\nRight now, the greatest chances (30-50%) look to occur Thursday\nmorning, then during the heat of the afternoon and evening on\nThursday, Friday, and Saturday. There will be plenty of dry\nhours during this time, and not everyone will see rain. In \nterms of any potentially severe thunderstorms, the longer range \nCAMs show about 25-35 kts of 0-6 km bulk shear across SE MO and \nSW IL Thursday afternoon, so an isolated severe storm with \ndamaging wind gusts cannot be ruled out. \n\nBy Sunday into next week, the frontal boundary will retreat to\nthe north as H5 ridging across the Desert Southwest expands\neastward into the central/southern Great Plains and Ozarks. This\nwill mean increasing temperature and humidity levels, beginning\nSunday and continuing into early next week. Right now, high\ntemperatures are forecast to reach the lower 90s with dew point\ntemperatures in the middle to upper 70s. This will yield heat\nindex values of 100-105 degrees. Since the region (especially\nsouthwest IN, northwest KY, and the Pennyrile) will be on the\neastern flank of the ridge, we will need to watch for potential\n\"ridge-rider\" thunderstorm clusters as well during this period,\nbut confidence is low at this time range.",
